      <section class="section" id="intro">
        <div class="section-label">Module 01</div>
        <h2>Fundamentals: The Deception Landscape</h2>
        <p>In the vast ocean of cyber threats, phishing remains the most persistent and successful predator. But what
          exactly is it? At its core, <strong>phishing is a type of social engineering</strong> where an attacker sends
          a fraudulent message designed to trick a person into revealing sensitive information or deploying malicious
          software on the victim's infrastructure.</p>

        <h3>The "Fishing" Analogy</h3>
        <p>The term is a deliberate play on words. Imagine a fisherman casting a line into a lake. He doesn't know which
          fish will bite, but he knows that if he uses the right lure and stays patient, eventually, a fish will mistake
          the lure for food and get hooked. In digital phishing:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The Lure:</strong> The deceptive email, SMS, or website.</li>
          <li><strong>The Hook:</strong> The malicious link or attachment.</li>
          <li><strong>The Fisherman:</strong> The cybercriminal seeking your credentials, money, or data.</li>
        </ul>

