      <!-- SECTION 2 -->
      <section class="section" id="history">
        <div class="section-label">Module 02</div>
        <h2>Historical Evolution: From AOL to AI</h2>
        <p>Phishing isn't new. It has evolved in lockstep with the internet itself, transforming from crude, misspelled
          notes to sophisticated, AI-driven campaigns.</p>

        <h3>The 1990s: The AOL Era and the Birth of "Phishing"</h3>
        <p>The first recorded use of the term "phishing" occurred in 1996 in the Usenet newsgroup <em>AOHell</em>.
          Attackers used "spoofed" screen names to message AOL users, pretending to be AOL employees. They would say
          things like, "We need to verify your billing information," or "Your account has a security issue."
          Unsuspecting users would hand over their credit card numbers or passwords, and the era of digital fraud began.
          At this time, the attacks were elementary; they relied entirely on the novelty of the internet. Most users
          didn't even know that "spoofing" was possible. The community was built on trust, which made it a fertile
          ground for the first generation of cyber-scammers.</p>

        <h3>The 2000s: Financial Exploitation and the Rise of Web Proxies</h3>
        <p>As online banking became mainstream, phishers moved to where the money was. We saw massive campaigns
          targeting customers of eBay, PayPal, and major banks like Citibank and Barclays. This era introduced the
          "clone website"—a near-perfect replica of a legitimate login page hosted on a slightly misspelled domain.
          Attackers began using automated toolkits, allowing even non-technical criminals to launch sophisticated
          attacks. They also started using "Fast-Flux" DNS techniques to hide their infrastructure, making it harder for
          law enforcement to shut down the fake sites. The introduction of the "Rock Phish" kit in 2006 changed the game
          by allowing one server to host multiple phishing brands at once.</p>

        <h3>The 2010s: Targeted Precision, Social Media, and BEC</h3>
        <p>Criminals realized that "spraying and praying" (sending millions of emails to random targets) was becoming
          less effective due to improved spam filters like Google's Gmail filters. They pivoted to <strong>Spear
            Phishing</strong>—highly targeted attacks based on research about the victim. The rise of social media
          (LinkedIn, Facebook) provided a treasure trove of data. This led to the rise of Business Email Compromise
          (BEC). In these attacks, criminals wouldn't even use malware; they would simply use the power of impersonation
          to convince a CFO to wire millions of dollars to a "new" vendor account. Organizations like FACC and Ubiquiti
          Networks lost tens of millions during this decade.</p>

        <h3>The 2020s: The AI Frontier and Deepfakes</h3>
        <p>Today, we are in the era of <strong>AI-enhanced phishing</strong>. Attackers use Large Language Models (LLMs)
          to generate perfect, professional prose in any language, eliminating the "grammar errors" that once served as
          red flags for English speakers. Furthermore, "Phishing-as-a-Service" (PhaaS) has emerged on the dark web,
          where professional developers rent out their phishing infrastructure to low-skill criminals for a monthly
          subscription. We are also seeing the dawn of <strong>vishing 2.0</strong>, where deepfake audio is used to
          impersonate the voice of a CEO during a phone call, instructing an employee to authorize a fraudulent payment.
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-info">
          <span class="alert-label">DID YOU KNOW?</span>
          The famous "Nigerian Prince" scam (419 scam) predates email! It originated as physical letters sent via mail
          in the 1980s, eventually migrating to fax and then to the emails we recognize today.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 3 -->
      <section class="section" id="types">
        <div class="section-label">Module 03</div>
        <h2>Classifying the Threat: Types of Phishing</h2>
        <p>Not all phishing is the same. Understanding the different categories is crucial for an investigator to
          determine the attacker's motive and sophistication level.</p>

        <div class="steps">
          <li>
            <div class="step-content">
              <strong>Mass Phishing (Spray &amp; Pray)</strong>
              The most common type. These are generic emails sent to millions of people simultaneously. The goal is
              volume; if only 0.01% of people click, the campaign is a success.
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-content">
              <strong>Spear Phishing</strong>
              A targeted attack directed at a specific individual, office, or organization. The attacker researches the
              target (often via LinkedIn or social media) to make the email seem incredibly relevant and personal.
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-content">
              <strong>Whaling (The Big Catch)</strong>
              A form of spear phishing specifically targeting high-level executives (CEOs, CFOs, Board Members). Because
              these individuals have high-level access, compromises are devastating.
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-content">
              <strong>Vishing (Voice Phishing)</strong>
              Phishing over the phone. You might receive a call from an automated voice or a real person pretending to
              be from "Microsoft Support" or "The Tax Office."
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-content">
              <strong>Smishing (SMS Phishing)</strong>
              Phishing via text messages. These often contain urgent links about "missed deliveries" or "unauthorized
              bank transfers" to trick you into clicking from your mobile device.
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-content">
              <strong>BEC (Business Email Compromise)</strong>
              A sophisticated scam where an attacker impersonates a company executive or a trusted vendor to trick an
              employee into performing a wire transfer or sharing sensitive corporate data.
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-content">
              <strong>Angler Phishing</strong>
              Attackers create fake social media accounts that look like customer service for a brand. When a customer
              tweets a complaint, the "Angler" replies with a malicious link to "help" them resolve the issue.
            </div>
          </li>
        </div>

