      <section class="section" id="headers">
        <div class="section-label">Module 05</div>
        <h2>Forensic Investigation: Header Analysis</h2>
        <p>The body of an email is the "skin," but the <strong>Internet Headers</strong> are the "DNA." They tell the
          story of where the email actually came from, regardless of what the "From" line says.</p>

        <h3>How to View Headers</h3>
        <p>Every email client allows you to view the "Original" or "Raw" message. This will look like a wall of text,
          but specific fields are goldmines for investigators.</p>

        <pre><span class="com"># Sample Header Snippet Analysis</span>
<span class="key">Delivered-To:</span> victim@example.com
<span class="key">Received:</span> from mail-ej1-x62c.google.com (mail-ej1-x62c.google.com. [209.85.218.44])
        by mx.google.com with SMTPS id ...
<span class="key">Return-Path:</span> <span class="high"><attacker-outbox@scamserver.cc></attacker-outbox@scamserver.cc></span> <span class="com"># REAL ORIGIN</span>
<span class="key">From:</span> "CEO of Your Bank" <support@yourbank.com> <span class="com"># THE LIES</span>
<span class="key">Authentication-Results:</span> speed.google.com;
       spf=pass (google.com: domain of ...);
       <span class="key">dkim=fail</span> <span class="high">(reason="signature verification failed")</span>
       <span class="key">dmarc=fail</span> <span class="com"># THE TRUTH</span></support@yourbank.com></pre>

        <div class="steps">
          <li>
            <div class="step-content">
              <strong>Return-Path</strong>
              This is the address where bounce messages (delivery failures) are sent. Attackers often forget to spoof
              this, revealing their actual automated mailer address.
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-content">
              <strong>Received-From</strong>
              This shows the path the email took across the internet. Like breadcrumbs, it shows the IP addresses of
              every mail server the message touched.
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-content">
              <strong>Authentication Checks (SPF, DKIM, DMARC)</strong>
              These are security protocols that verify a sender's identity. If you see <code>dkim=fail</code> or
              <code>dmarc=fail</code>, the email is almost certainly fraudulent.
            </div>
          </li>
        </div>

        <div class="visual-box">
          <p style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono'; font-size:11px; text-align:left; color:var(--text-secondary);">
            +-------------------+ +-------------------+ +------------------+<br>
            | Attacker Server | ----&gt; | Relaying Server | ----&gt; | Victim's Mailbox |<br>
            | IP: 104.21.1.8 | | IP: 209.85.218.44 | | IP: 172.5.12.98 |<br>
            +-------------------+ +-------------------+ +------------------+<br>
            &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;| |<br>
            &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;+--- Headers record this ---+
          </p>
          <div class="diagram-label">The Mail Transfer Path</div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section class="section" id="payload">
        <div class="section-label">Module 06</div>
        <h2>Payload Analysis: Links &amp; Attachments</h2>
        <p>The "Payload" is the part of the attack that actually does the damage. As an investigator, you must handle
          these with extreme caution.</p>

        <h3>De-fanging Indicators</h3>
        <p>When writing a report about a malicious URL or IP, never write it in a clickable format. If you do, a
          colleague might accidentally click it and get infected. We "de-fang" them by adding brackets.</p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Indicator Type</th>
                <th>Dangerous Format</th>
                <th>De-fanged Format</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>URL</td>
                <td>https://malicious.com/virus</td>
                <td>hxxps://malicious[.]com/virus</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>IP Address</td>
                <td>123.45.67.89</td>
                <td>123[.]45[.]67[.]89</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Domain</td>
                <td>evil-site.net</td>
                <td>evil-site[.]net</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3>Attachment Analysis &amp; Sandboxing</h3>
        <p>Never open an attachment on your base OS. Use these steps instead:</p>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            <div class="step-content">
              <strong>Generate a Hash:</strong> Use a tool to create a digital fingerprint (MD5, SHA256) of the file.
              <pre><code>sha256sum suspicious_invoice.xlsx</code></pre>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-content">
              <strong>Check Threat Intelligence:</strong> Search for that hash on sites like <strong>VirusTotal</strong>
              or <strong>Hybrid Analysis</strong>. If someone else has seen this malware, you'll know instantly.
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-content">
              <strong>Dynamic Analysis (Sandboxing):</strong> Upload the file to a secure, isolated cloud environment (a
              sandbox) where the file can run safely while sensors record its behavior. You might see the "invoice"
              trying to connect to a server in a foreign country to download ransomware.
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 7 -->
      <section class="section" id="casestudies">
        <div class="section-label">Module 07</div>
        <h2>Real-World Case Studies</h2>
        <p>Examining past failures is the best way to learn. These cases highlight how simple deception can lead to
          multi-million dollar losses.</p>

        <div class="case-study">
          <h3>1. The FACC BEC Scam (2016)</h3>
          <p>FACC, an Austrian aerospace parts manufacturer, lost <strong>€42 million</strong> ($50M+) in a single
            phishing attack. The attacker sent an email to the accounting department impersonating the CEO, claiming
            there was a "secret acquisition" and requesting an urgent wire transfer to a specific account. Because the
            email was so convincing and utilized the CEO's authority, the money was sent without further verification.
            The company's CEO and CFO were both fired as a result.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="case-study">
          <h3>2. The Google/Facebook Invoicing Fraud (2013-2015)</h3>
          <p>A single individual managed to steal over <strong>$100 million</strong> from Google and Facebook. How? He
            set up a fake company with the same name as "Quanta Computer," a legitimate hardware supplier for both tech
            giants. He then sent fraudulent invoices to their accounting departments. Because the tech companies were
            used to seeing bills from "Quanta," they paid the fake ones for years before the fraud was discovered.</p>
          <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Always verify the bank account details of your vendors, especially if they claim
            to have "changed" their payment information recently.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="case-study">
          <h3>3. The John Podesta Hack (2016)</h3>
          <p>This is perhaps the most famous case of spear phishing in political history. John Podesta, the chairman of
            Hillary Clinton's 2016 presidential campaign, received a "security alert" from Google saying a hacker had
            his password. The email contained a "Change password" button. Podesta's IT aide accidentally told him the
            email was "legitimate" (he meant to type "illegitimate"). Podesta clicked the link, typed his password into
            the attacker's fake page, and thousands of his emails were stolen and leaked.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 8 -->
      <section class="section" id="mitigation">
        <div class="section-label">Module 08</div>
        <h2>Defense &amp; Response Strategies</h2>
        <p>Phishing is a battle of technology and education. Here is how organizations can fight back.</p>

        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">🛡️ The Multi-Layered Defense</div>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Technical Controls:</strong> Email filters that block known malicious IPs and scan attachments
              in real-time.</li>
            <li><strong>MFA (Multi-Factor Authentication):</strong> The single most effective defense. Even if a phisher
              steals your password, they can't log in without your physical security key or phone code.</li>
            <li><strong>Domain Monitoring:</strong> Use services that alert you when someone registers a domain that
              looks like your brand (e.g., <code>y0urbank.com</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Employee Training:</strong> Conduct "simulated phishing" tests. Send a safe "test" phish to
              employees; if they click, they get immediate training on what they missed.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3>Responding to a Breach</h3>
        <p>If you or a user clicks a phishing link, time is critical:</p>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li><strong>Isolate:</strong> Disconnect the infected device from the network immediately.</li>
          <li><strong>Reset:</strong> Rotate passwords for 모든 accounts, assuming they are compromised.</li>
          <li><strong>Investigate:</strong> Analyze the email to find out what else the attacker might have done.</li>
          <li><strong>Communicate:</strong> Notify IT security and, if necessary, the legal/PR department.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>
